1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,391
Can you add every number from one to
one hundred, in less than ten seconds?

2
00:00:05,791 --> 00:00:08,008
Diya tried it at the study table.

3
00:00:08,008 --> 00:00:09,681
One plus two plus three.

4
00:00:09,681 --> 00:00:11,057
It was taking ages.

5
00:00:11,507 --> 00:00:15,086
A famous story says young Carl Friedrich
Gauss did a sum like this in moments.

6
00:00:15,436 --> 00:00:16,610
No calculator.

7
00:00:16,610 --> 00:00:19,088
Just one clever idea about pairs.

8
00:00:19,588 --> 00:00:23,203
That idea gives us the sum
of an arithmetic progression.

9
00:00:23,853 --> 00:00:24,973
Quick reminder.

10
00:00:24,973 --> 00:00:29,493
An arithmetic progression, or AP,
adds the same number every time.

11
00:00:29,893 --> 00:00:31,960
Like saving twenty rupees every week.

12
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:33,843
Twenty, forty, sixty, eighty.

13
00:00:34,243 --> 00:00:36,048
The first term is called a.

14
00:00:36,048 --> 00:00:39,409
The number we keep adding
is d, the common difference.

15
00:00:39,809 --> 00:00:43,502
To reach term n, we add
d only n minus one times.

16
00:00:43,502 --> 00:00:46,177
So term n is a plus n minus one d.

17
00:00:46,777 --> 00:00:50,811
This sum is a Class 10 topic, so the
Class 11 NCERT text now skips it.

18
00:00:50,811 --> 00:00:52,237
JEE Main still asks it.

19
00:00:52,837 --> 00:00:53,877
Now the trick.

20
00:00:53,877 --> 00:00:57,097
Write the sum forwards,
from one up to one hundred.

21
00:00:57,497 --> 00:01:01,782
Under it, write the same sum backwards,
from one hundred down to one.

22
00:01:02,182 --> 00:01:03,339
Add each column.

23
00:01:03,339 --> 00:01:06,152
One plus one hundred
is one hundred and one.

24
00:01:06,152 --> 00:01:09,248
Two plus ninety nine,
again one hundred and one.

25
00:01:09,648 --> 00:01:13,428
Every column makes one hundred and
one, and there are one hundred columns.

26
00:01:13,828 --> 00:01:17,616
So both rows make one hundred
times one hundred and one.

27
00:01:17,616 --> 00:01:19,393
Ten thousand one hundred.

28
00:01:19,793 --> 00:01:21,769
But we wrote the sum twice.

29
00:01:21,769 --> 00:01:23,281
So one sum is half.

30
00:01:23,281 --> 00:01:24,984
Five thousand and fifty.

31
00:01:25,634 --> 00:01:28,054
Here is the same trick as a picture.

32
00:01:28,054 --> 00:01:30,846
A staircase of blocks: one, two, three.

33
00:01:31,246 --> 00:01:34,352
Turn a copy upside down and fit it on top.

34
00:01:34,352 --> 00:01:38,110
Now it is a rectangle, n
wide and n plus one tall.

35
00:01:38,510 --> 00:01:39,578
Watch n grow.

36
00:01:39,578 --> 00:01:42,369
One staircase is always
half the rectangle.

37
00:01:42,769 --> 00:01:47,876
So one plus two, up to n, is
n times n plus one, over two.

38
00:01:48,526 --> 00:01:49,665
Now any AP.

39
00:01:49,665 --> 00:01:52,216
Its sum of n terms is called S n.

40
00:01:52,216 --> 00:01:54,213
Its last term is called l.

41
00:01:54,613 --> 00:01:55,513
Step one.

42
00:01:55,481 --> 00:01:57,004
Write S n forwards.

43
00:01:57,004 --> 00:01:59,363
a, then a plus d, up to l.

44
00:02:00,113 --> 00:02:01,013
Step two.

45
00:02:00,952 --> 00:02:02,364
Write it backwards.

46
00:02:02,364 --> 00:02:04,800
l, then l minus d, down to a.

47
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:06,494
Step three.

48
00:02:06,494 --> 00:02:07,602
Add the rows.

49
00:02:07,602 --> 00:02:11,163
One term goes up by d,
the other goes down by d.

50
00:02:11,163 --> 00:02:13,287
So every column is a plus l.

51
00:02:14,037 --> 00:02:14,937
Step four.

52
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:16,214
There are n columns.

53
00:02:16,214 --> 00:02:18,612
So two S n equals n times a plus l.

54
00:02:19,362 --> 00:02:20,343
Step five.

55
00:02:20,343 --> 00:02:21,618
Divide by two.

56
00:02:21,618 --> 00:02:24,832
S n equals n over two, times a plus l.

57
00:02:25,582 --> 00:02:28,043
And l is a plus n minus one d.

58
00:02:28,043 --> 00:02:33,500
Put that in, and S n equals n over
two, times two a plus n minus one d.

59
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:35,276
Side by side.

60
00:02:35,276 --> 00:02:37,575
The first form needs the last term.

61
00:02:37,975 --> 00:02:41,128
The second form needs the
common difference instead.

62
00:02:41,728 --> 00:02:42,628
Your turn.

63
00:02:42,561 --> 00:02:45,298
Three, seven, eleven, for twenty terms.

64
00:02:45,298 --> 00:02:46,819
Which form is quicker?

65
00:02:46,819 --> 00:02:47,997
Pause and think.

66
00:02:51,197 --> 00:02:52,219
The second one.

67
00:02:52,219 --> 00:02:55,674
We know d is four, but we do
not know the last term yet.

68
00:02:56,324 --> 00:02:57,315
Remember this.

69
00:02:57,315 --> 00:03:01,526
The sum is the number of terms, times
the average of the first and the

70
00:03:01,526 --> 00:03:02,426
last.

71
00:03:02,744 --> 00:03:04,505
That average has a name.

72
00:03:04,505 --> 00:03:06,628
The arithmetic mean, or AM.

73
00:03:07,028 --> 00:03:10,750
The arithmetic mean of a
and b is a plus b, over two.

74
00:03:10,750 --> 00:03:13,172
It sits exactly halfway between them.

75
00:03:13,572 --> 00:03:15,535
Like meeting a friend halfway.

76
00:03:15,535 --> 00:03:18,292
You stand at four, she stands at sixteen.

77
00:03:18,292 --> 00:03:19,527
You meet at ten.

78
00:03:19,527 --> 00:03:21,997
And four, ten, sixteen is an AP.

79
00:03:22,597 --> 00:03:24,620
We can fit in more numbers.

80
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:29,751
To put k means between a and b, cut
the gap into k plus one equal steps.

81
00:03:30,151 --> 00:03:31,467
One mean gives ten.

82
00:03:31,467 --> 00:03:33,488
Two means give eight and twelve.

83
00:03:33,488 --> 00:03:36,065
Three means give seven, ten and thirteen.

84
00:03:36,715 --> 00:03:37,615
Quick one.

85
00:03:37,497 --> 00:03:40,273
What is the arithmetic
mean of six and twenty?

86
00:03:40,273 --> 00:03:41,378
Pause and think.

87
00:03:44,578 --> 00:03:45,478
Thirteen.

88
00:03:45,307 --> 00:03:49,210
Six plus twenty is twenty six,
and half of that is thirteen.

89
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:51,550
Now, one worked example.

90
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:56,122
Find the sum of the first twenty
terms of three, seven, eleven, and so

91
00:03:56,122 --> 00:03:57,022
on.

92
00:03:57,036 --> 00:03:57,936
Step one.

93
00:03:57,871 --> 00:03:58,869
a is three.

94
00:03:58,869 --> 00:04:01,649
d is seven minus three, which is four.

95
00:04:01,649 --> 00:04:02,963
And n is twenty.

96
00:04:03,713 --> 00:04:04,613
Step two.

97
00:04:04,557 --> 00:04:08,637
S twenty is twenty over two,
times six plus nineteen fours.

98
00:04:09,387 --> 00:04:10,287
Step three.

99
00:04:10,243 --> 00:04:12,244
Nineteen fours make seventy six.

100
00:04:12,244 --> 00:04:14,311
So we get ten times eighty two.

101
00:04:15,061 --> 00:04:15,961
Step four.

102
00:04:15,783 --> 00:04:17,835
The sum is eight hundred and twenty.

103
00:04:18,585 --> 00:04:19,485
Check.

104
00:04:19,194 --> 00:04:23,919
Twenty times forty one, the average
of three and seventy nine, gives eight

105
00:04:23,919 --> 00:04:25,297
hundred and twenty.

106
00:04:25,947 --> 00:04:27,409
Now, the exam trap.

107
00:04:27,759 --> 00:04:30,480
Add the odd numbers
from one to ninety nine.

108
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:32,728
Many students take n as ninety nine.

109
00:04:33,128 --> 00:04:35,419
Only every second number is odd.

110
00:04:35,419 --> 00:04:40,302
Ninety nine is one plus forty nine
twos, so n is fifty, and the sum is

111
00:04:40,302 --> 00:04:42,158
two thousand five hundred.

112
00:04:42,808 --> 00:04:44,252
Where is this asked?

113
00:04:44,252 --> 00:04:48,797
JEE Main, January twenty nineteen:
two sums of an AP are linked, find

114
00:04:48,797 --> 00:04:49,941
its tenth term.

115
00:04:50,541 --> 00:04:51,784
Exam level now.

116
00:04:51,784 --> 00:04:56,169
The sum of n terms of an AP
is three n squared plus two n.

117
00:04:56,169 --> 00:04:57,740
Find the tenth term.

118
00:04:57,740 --> 00:04:59,036
Pause and solve.

119
00:05:04,436 --> 00:05:05,956
The answer is B.

120
00:05:05,956 --> 00:05:07,040
Fifty nine.

121
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:12,234
The tenth term is the sum of ten
terms, minus the sum of nine terms.

122
00:05:12,734 --> 00:05:15,155
S ten is three hundred and twenty.

123
00:05:15,155 --> 00:05:17,739
S nine is two hundred and sixty one.

124
00:05:18,239 --> 00:05:19,691
Subtract: fifty nine.

125
00:05:19,691 --> 00:05:21,161
Option D is the trap.

126
00:05:21,161 --> 00:05:23,248
That is the sum, not the term.

127
00:05:23,848 --> 00:05:25,388
Here is your recap.

128
00:05:25,388 --> 00:05:26,970
Screenshot this card.

129
00:05:30,170 --> 00:05:32,356
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

130
00:05:32,756 --> 00:05:33,656
One.

131
00:05:33,211 --> 00:05:36,067
Why does every column
add up to the same total?

132
00:05:36,967 --> 00:05:37,867
Two.

133
00:05:37,416 --> 00:05:40,229
Find the sum of the first
fifty natural numbers.

134
00:05:41,129 --> 00:05:42,029
Three.

135
00:05:41,681 --> 00:05:44,832
Insert two arithmetic means
between three and eighteen.

136
00:05:45,932 --> 00:05:48,772
Next time: a rumour
that doubles every hour.

137
00:05:48,772 --> 00:05:50,394
Multiplying, not adding.

138
00:05:50,394 --> 00:05:52,016
The geometric progression.
